## Migration: EventProcessor (.NET Framework → .NET 8)

### Critical Issues in the Original Code

1. **`Thread.Abort()`** — throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 8 (removed since .NET 5)
2. **`Thread.Interrupt()`** — throws `ThreadInterruptedException` but is unreliable and [obsolete since .NET 7](https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/core/compatibility/core-libraries/7.0/thread-interrupt) (`SYSLIB0056`)
3. **Race condition** — multiple `Enqueue` calls between `WaitOne`/`Reset` lose items (only one `Dequeue` per signal)
4. **Manual `Thread` + `ManualResetEvent` + `lock`** — replaced by `Channel<T>` or `BlockingCollection<T>` in modern .NET

### Migrated Code (using `Channel<T>` + `CancellationToken`)

```csharp
using System.Threading.Channels;

public class EventProcessor : IAsyncDisposable
{
    private readonly Channel<string> _channel = Channel.CreateUnbounded<string>();
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private Task? _processTask;

    public void Start()
    {
        _processTask = Task.Run(() => RunLoopAsync(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task RunLoopAsync(CancellationToken ct)
    {
        try
        {
            await foreach (var item in _channel.Reader.ReadAllAsync(ct))
            {
                Handle(item);
            }
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (ct.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            // Clean shutdown — drain remaining items if desired
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        if (!_channel.Writer.TryWrite(item))
            throw new InvalidOperationException("Channel closed.");
    }

    public async ValueTask StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        _channel.Writer.Complete();           // signal no more items
        _cts.CancelAfter(timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5)); // hard deadline

        if (_processTask is not null)
            await _processTask;
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        await StopAsync();
        _cts.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { /* your logic */ }
}
```

### Key Migration Decisions

| .NET Framework | .NET 8 Replacement | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `Thread` + `ManualResetEvent` | `Task.Run` + `Channel<T>` | No manual signaling/locking needed |
| `Thread.Abort()` | `CancellationToken` | Cooperative cancellation — safe, no corrupted state |
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | `Channel.Writer.Complete()` | Graceful "no more work" signal |
| `lock` + `Queue<T>` | `Channel<T>` (thread-safe) | Lock-free, bounded/unbounded, built-in backpressure |

### Pitfalls to Watch For

1. **`Thread.Abort` silently does nothing** in .NET 6–7 (no exception), then **throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** in .NET 8+. Your `Stop()` method would hang at `Join(5000)` then crash.

2. **Don't just swap `Thread.Abort` → `Thread.Interrupt`** — `Interrupt` only works if the thread is in a `WaitSleepJoin` state, and it's itself obsolete.

3. **The original race condition**: two rapid `Enqueue` calls → `Set()` called twice, but `WaitOne`/`Reset`/`Dequeue` only processes one item. `Channel<T>` eliminates this entirely.

4. **Backpressure**: use `Channel.CreateBounded<T>(capacity)` if producers can outpace the consumer — the original `Queue<T>` had no limit.

5. **Exception handling in `Handle()`**: the original code crashes the thread silently. Wrap `Handle()` in try/catch or use a fault-tolerant loop if one bad item shouldn't kill the processor.
